## Issue: "Add Widget" edit-mode button is a no-op — there is no UI path to add a widget

**File:** apps/apple/Sources/Features/Dashboard/DashboardPage.swift:188–190
**Severity:** Medium

**Problem:** The edit-mode "Add Widget" toolbar button is wired to `model.setEditMode(true)`:
```swift
Button(action: { model.setEditMode(true) }, label: {
    Label("dashboard.addWidget", systemImage: "plus")
})
```
This button only renders inside `editModeActions`, which only appears when `model.editMode == true` (DashboardPage.swift:172–177). So tapping it sets `editMode` to `true` while it is already `true` — a complete no-op. The user taps a prominent "+ Add Widget" control and nothing happens.

This is not just one mis-wired button: `DashboardPageModel.addWidget(_:)` (DashboardPageModel.swift:176) is never invoked from any view (verified by grep across `apps/apple/Sources/`), and there is no widget picker sheet, context menu, or add-tile anywhere in the feature. The customize-hint CTA also routes to `setEditMode(true)` (DashboardPage.swift:143), not to an add action. Net effect: once a user removes a widget in edit mode (via the tile's minus button), the **only** ways to get it back are Undo or Reset — there is no discoverable "add" path at all, even though the model fully supports it and it's unit-tested.

**Evidence:**
- `grep -rn "addWidget"` across `Sources/` returns only the model definition (line 176) and the `Label` text (line 189) — zero call sites.
- `grep` for `.sheet` / `Picker` / `contextMenu` / `Menu {` in the Dashboard feature returns nothing relevant.
- `editModeActions` is gated by `if model.editMode` (lines 172–177), so the action's `setEditMode(true)` cannot change state.

**Suggested fix:** Surface a real add path — e.g., present a sheet/menu of `DashboardWidget` cases not currently in `model.layout` and call `model.addWidget(_:)` on selection (mirroring web's `WidgetPicker`/catalogue). If an add UI is genuinely out of scope for this unit, the button should at least be disabled or removed rather than presenting a labeled control that does nothing.

---

## Issue: Templates → "New Dashboard" silently resets the layout to default

**File:** apps/apple/Sources/Features/Dashboard/DashboardPage.swift:70–72
**Severity:** Low

**Problem:**
```swift
.confirmationDialog("dashboard.templates", isPresented: $showTemplates, titleVisibility: .visible) {
    Button("dashboard.newDashboard") { model.resetToDefault() }
}
```
The "New Dashboard" action calls `model.resetToDefault()` — identical to the "Reset" action (line 66). So a control labeled "New Dashboard" actually restores the 8 seeded widgets and discards the user's current customization. Notably, the dedicated Reset path guards this behind a confirmation dialog showing `layout.resetMessage` ("This removes all customizations…"), but this "New Dashboard" button performs the same destructive layout replacement with no such warning. In the web source, the blank template invokes `createDashboard(...)` (a new empty layout), not a reset.

**Evidence:** Both `Button("dashboard.newDashboard")` (line 71) and `Button("dashboard.reset", role: .destructive)` (line 66) invoke the same `model.resetToDefault()`. The native model has no create-dashboard concept, so the label and behavior don't align.

**Suggested fix:** Since there's no multi-dashboard model natively, either relabel the action to reflect that it restores the default layout, or route it through the same confirmation used by Reset so the customization-loss is acknowledged. (It is at least undoable — `resetToDefault()` pushes undo history — which is why this is Low rather than higher.)

